# Swift Code Standards

## Swift Style Guide
- **Follow Swift API Design Guidelines** (https://swift.org/documentation/api-design-guidelines/)
- Use meaningful, descriptive variable and function names
- Prefer `let` over `var` when possible (immutability)
- Use guard statements for early returns
- Use camelCase for variables/functions, PascalCase for types
- Use 4 spaces for indentation (not tabs)
- Limit line length to 100 characters (soft limit, 120 max)

## Naming Conventions
- **Descriptive names:** `calculateTotalPrice()` not `calcTP()`
- **Clear intent:** `isColorPickerActive` not `flag`
- **Consistent terminology:** Use same terms throughout codebase
- **Avoid abbreviations:** `color` not `clr`, `manager` not `mgr`
- **Boolean properties:** Use `is`, `has`, `can` prefixes

## Code Organization

### File Structure
- One primary type per file (class, struct, enum)
- File name matches type name exactly
- Use extensions to organize code within files
- Group related functionality together

### Extension Organization
```swift
// Main type definition
class ColorPickerController {
    // Core properties and methods
}

// MARK: - Protocol Conformances
extension ColorPickerController: NSWindowDelegate {
    // Protocol methods
}

// MARK: - Private Helpers
private extension ColorPickerController {
    // Private helper methods
}
```

### Directory Structure
- **App/**: Application entry point and app delegate
- **Controllers/**: View controllers and managers
- **Models/**: Data models and business logic
- **Views/**: UI components and custom views
- **Utilities/**: Helper classes and utilities
- **Resources/**: Assets, Info.plist, etc.

## Architecture Patterns
- **MVC:** Use Model-View-Controller pattern
- **Separation of Concerns:** Keep controllers, models, utilities separate
- **Single Responsibility:** Each class should have one clear purpose
- **Dependency Injection:** Avoid singletons where possible
- **Protocol-Oriented:** Use protocols for abstraction and testability

## Code Quality

### Clarity Over Cleverness
- Write code that's easy to understand
- Favor explicit over implicit
- Make intent clear through naming and structure

### Error Handling
- Always use try-catch blocks for error-prone operations
- Use Result types for operations that can fail
- Log errors to console with clear messages
- Handle errors gracefully - don't crash the app
- Provide user-friendly error messages when appropriate

### Type Safety
- Use strong typing, avoid `Any` when possible
- Use optionals appropriately
- Leverage Swift's type system for safety

### Performance
- Profile before optimizing
- Use lazy properties for expensive initialization
- Cache expensive computations
- Avoid unnecessary allocations in hot paths

## Open Source Best Practices

### Code Clarity
- Write self-documenting code
- Use meaningful names that explain purpose
- Structure code logically
- Add comments for WHY, not WHAT

### Modularity
- Clear module boundaries
- Well-defined interfaces
- Minimal coupling between modules
- High cohesion within modules

### Consistency
- Follow established patterns in the codebase
- Use consistent formatting
- Maintain consistent naming conventions
- Follow the same architectural patterns

## Code Review Checklist
Before submitting code for review:
- [ ] Follows Swift API Design Guidelines
- [ ] No linter errors
- [ ] All tests passing
- [ ] Performance targets met
- [ ] Documentation updated (public APIs documented)
- [ ] Error handling implemented
- [ ] Memory leaks checked
- [ ] File size under 400 lines
- [ ] Code is clear and self-documenting
- [ ] Naming is descriptive and consistent
